  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1963-p40.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let \\(u=\\sqrt[3]{x+9}\\) and \\(v=\\sqrt[3]{x-9}.\\) Then \\(u-v=3\\) and \\[ \\begin{aligned} 18&=u^3-v^3\\\\ &=(u-v)(u^2+uv+v^2), \\end{aligned} \\] so \\(u^2+uv+v^2=6.\\) Comparing with \\((u-v)^2=9\\) gives \\(uv=-1.\\) Hence \\((u+v)^2=5.\\) Cubing \\(2u=3\\pm\\sqrt5\\) gives \\(x=\\pm4\\sqrt5,\\) and either way \\(x^2=80,\\) between \\(75\\) and \\(85.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Set \\(u=\\sqrt[3]{x+9}\\) and \\(v=\\sqrt[3]{x-9}\\)",
        "Use both \\(u-v=3\\) and \\(u^3-v^3=18\\) to find \\(uv\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
